    58	
    59	He looked at her for a moment longer than he looked at anyone else. Then he put her with the faces and let her go.
    60	
    61	At the quarter bell Dace's shadow fell across the arch and stayed there. He never set foot in a fighter's alcove before a bout, by some rule of his own that he had never explained to anybody. He leaned one shoulder on the stone with his hands in his pockets, and on his face was the look he wore in front of his wall on the rare morning when every slot was filled and nobody had smudged the chalk.
    62	
    63	"It's yours till it's finished," he said. "No card after. No card before. I've told them all to go home after it, and they won't, but I've told them."
    64	
