editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:85:There was one thing left that he could do about it, and it was to do nothing. He did not write again, or go down to the long room to look at the file. When the river-side returns came up on the Thursday tray for information, he put his thumb on the routing card and signed nothing, as he had always done. If anybody ever went through his log they would find one line, one note, one date, and then nothing at all, which was the most a man could say in that building without saying anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-35.md:97:Once, late in the month, he went further back than that. He asked the clerk in the long room for three files of his own, from his first years, which he had closed long ago and not thought of since: a carter, a widow's appeal, a practitioner who had moved to the coast. He told himself he wanted to check his old hand. He turned to their classification blocks under the counter's lamp, one after another, and found his own initials in every right-hand column, younger and rounder, beside every line he had ever entered. He stood there a while with the third file open. He did not know whether what he felt was relief that his own work was clean, or shame that it had taken him eleven years and a junior's question to go and look.
