editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:He had spent a week after the back bench trying to make the read last while Brom moved, because he wanted it to last; he had sat a whole card and a whole sweeping still enough to feel somebody at the far edge of a room. Somewhere under that he had begun to want, very badly, a version of the thing that would be worth something on a floor, against a man who did not stand still to be found. So every morning in the alcove he opened the door sitting, and Brom stood up, and took one slow step, and the door went out. He opened it again, and Brom lifted a hand, and it went out. Brom walked a slow circle round him at three paces and it went out on the first stride, every time, forty times, sixty, while the band came and went and came.
