editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:35:They did it all that morning, and the next, and the next, and he kept a tally on the back of his hand in ink because his head would not hold one. Most of the knocks brought back nothing but the smear, the wet-window blur that told him there was something warm in front of him and nothing else, which he could have had from his eyes for free. Perhaps one in three brought back the other thing, the weight and its leaning, sharp and certain for an instant. At a forearm it worked, at a pace it worked worse, and at two paces he got nothing but noise. Each knock cost a little band of its own, and they stacked, so that by the twentieth his head was a drum and he had to stop.
