editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:53:"There's a thing you said in the back room, my first fortnight," he said. "About the books at the bottom of the cupboard. That they don't talk like yours. You told me it would take an afternoon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:61:She sat back and looked at him over her spectacles for a long time. He thought she was going to say *sometime* again, as she had in the back room in his first fortnight, and he had decided on the walk down that if she did he would say *all right* and mean it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:"I know you know. That's not why I'm telling you." She put the pen down. "You carried my lamp, that first time, and didn't touch anything. You stood at this table a few weeks ago with your book and read it out in front of forty people, and every line was dated. And you stood on that bench the night of the red lines and watched me do it, and asked me after whether it was worth it." She looked at him. "That's three things, and I don't need four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:75:"Hands first," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:91:"And one book open at a time. Always. If you want another, this one's shut and back on its shelf before the next one comes down." She looked at him. "If a draught comes in at that door, or you knock the table with your knee, or you sneeze, it's one book that suffers. Not three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:Her own books first, along the wall. She took down one from near the end of the row, from her first years, and opened it on the table, and then one from ten years ago, and one from last season, and laid them side by side, open, square. Three books open at once: he looked at her, and she said, "Mine. I'll be careful with mine as I like." He looked at the three hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:109:His were in the cupboard, on the upper shelves: thirty-odd volumes, she said, she had never counted them properly because it seemed rude. They were in a rounder hand than hers, with bigger loops and a habit of writing the result first, in the margin, before the names, as though he had always wanted to know how a thing came out before he cared who had done it. Under the round hand, in the earliest of them, was another, cramped and sloping, with *Copper* spelt with two *p*s. The one-armed keeper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:123:It was. That was the strange thing, once he saw it: it was a keeper's book, laid out the way Vell's were laid out, a line to a bout, the same order. Two names. The outcome. A short note. A line of witnesses at the foot of each night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:133:There were other things missing, things he had not known he would miss. Vell's book had a column, narrow, at the side of every line, where she wrote a fighter's Path and his formal tier if he had one, and left it empty if he had none. There was no such column here, and no Path written anywhere on the page; a fighter was his name, and what he had done. Where a keeper wanted to say more, he said it in the note, and what he said was what he had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:The name at the head of the line was gone. Damp had got to that corner of the page long ago and taken it, all but two letters at the end, which could have belonged to any name in the world. The opponent's name was whole, the outcome plain, a win, and the witnesses seven. The note in the line said, in the short forms, that the fighter's abilities matched nothing the keeper knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:143:And in the margin beside it, in a smaller hand than the line, cramped, the ink a little darker as if the keeper had pressed harder, there was a note.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:145:*Assessed per pre-registry terminology as [UNBOUND]. Abilities not matching any guild standard. Competed without incident.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:157:He took the grey book out of his coat, with his hands still clean and dry, and opened it on his knee below the table, away from the old page. In the smallest hand he owned, smaller than the tally in the corner, small enough that he would need the lamp to read it back, he copied the first sentence. He left the brackets off, because they were the keeper's and not his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:159:*Assessed per pre-registry terminology as UNBOUND.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:201:They were quiet for a while, and out on the main floor he could hear the sweepers come in, and the long sound of the first broom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:207:"Good." She took the lamp off its bracket, now that every book was shut. "That was the handling. You know how to touch them now. The reading's another thing. That's a long afternoon, a lot of long afternoons, and we'll do it properly some day, from the first page." She held the lamp up. "Not tonight. I'm old and it's late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:227:He thought about that for a long time, on the river wall, in the cold, because it was not like him any more. He had told Lira about the read through the wall, and about the watcher on the same day she asked. He had told Brom the whole of the Log. He had stood in Vell's back room and said the Compact falsified things out loud. He had spent a season learning that the things you keep shut cost you, and that some of them were about other people, who had a right to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:229:But this had not happened to anybody he knew. It was one word in a margin, and he did not know what it meant. If he said it aloud, to Lira at the kitchen table or to Brom between crosses, he would have to say why he had copied it. Then he would have to say what he thought it meant, and he did not think anything yet. He only felt it, and a feeling said aloud becomes a claim, whether you mean it to or not. He had a book for claims, and this was not one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:233:When he got in, the house was dark except for the kitchen, where the heavyset man's wife had left the lamp turned low for him, as she did. He went up past Lira's door, which was shut and quiet, and sat at the crate desk without lighting his own lamp. In the dark he could see the practice lamp in the north window over the alcoves, very small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:241:It was a week after that, on a night with no card and the house quiet, that he took Keth's pages out and read them through from the beginning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:243:He had not meant to; he had sat down at the crate desk to write up a side-floor afternoon and found that he had nothing to say about it; it had been a clean bout and a short one, a Blade from the salt end who had stood too square. So he had turned back through the grey book idly, the way you turn back through a letter you already know, and come to the page with *the finger* at its head, where Keth's name had been until the night of Dace's story, and *No reason for this page* underneath, and kept going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:245:There were a great many pages, more than he had known. There was the first, from the morning Dace had pointed him out, with the little finger off the grip and the cuts that finished long. Then the chosen count, the three cuts and the three counts and *The only one I've seen*. Then dawn after dawn in the newcomers' ring, a line or two each, in pencil, never more, on mornings when he had gone down early for no reason he had ever written down. *The finger shows the parry slow, then fast, then real.* *Watching feet again.* *Chained three cuts for the tall lad and the third was late.* There were pages from the side floors, from main-floor nights. There was a note from the night of the Brom bout about Keth on the end of the east bench, looking at Brom's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:247:Three months of it. He had never once sat down to watch Keth. He had only, every time Keth was in the room, let his eyes do what they did and let his pencil follow, and then turned the page and forgotten it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:253:Keth chose his count fresh every cut. That was the first thing anybody would write about him, and Cael had written it first, and it was true. But when Keth chained his cuts, two and then three in a run, as he did against anybody good, the choosing stopped for a moment between the second cut and the third. Cael had it on eleven pages, from eleven different weeks, written down as eleven different small things. *Third late.* *Feet stuck on the third.* *Didn't turn for the third, cut it square.* *Second finished long and he couldn't get the angle back.* Eleven times he had written it down, and every time he had thought it was that morning's fault, or the newcomer's, or Keth's tiredness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:255:It was not any of those; it was the same thing. At the end of the second cut in a chain, Keth's blade finished long, the way it always did, the way of a man who had learned on a bigger blade. For an instant after it finished, the angle was set, and the feet had not yet caught up, and there was nothing Keth could choose. Then the third cut came out of whatever the second had left him. It was the smallest of seams, a sliver of a beat, and it opened at a range that was no use to anybody standing in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:257:But it was there. It had been there eleven times. And Keth did not know it, Cael thought. He had watched Keth teach that chain to newcomers with his own hands, slowly, and he had never once slowed down there, at the seam. Nobody slows down at a place he cannot see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:261:He had not been studying Keth; he would have said so to anybody, and believed it. Keth was the line at Iron, a man who taught twelve-year-olds for nothing at dawn and crouched beside the ones who fell. He had asked only to be told after, and called it the price. And he was the man Cael had very nearly decided, against all his own rules about the people in the grey book, that he liked. He had no plan to fight him. He had no plan for him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:267:He did not tell Lira, or Brom in the alcove in the morning. He did not quite know why not, and he made himself look at that too, because it was a thing he would have asked of anybody else. It was not a secret, the way the word in his coat was a secret. It was only that, until he had looked again, it was a thing he had found out about a man he liked without the man's leave. He did not yet know what he meant to do with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:269:He shut the grey book. Through the window, down the hill, the practice lamp in the north window over the alcoves was lit. Somewhere in the district Keth was asleep, probably, a man who chose his count fresh every cut, and did not know there was a place where he could not.

